## What changed and what has no 1:1 equivalent

| Servlet original | WebFlux replacement |
|---|---|
| `SecurityFilterChain` / `HttpSecurity` | `SecurityWebFilterChain` / `ServerHttpSecurity` |
| `ClientRegistrationRepository` | `ReactiveClientRegistrationRepository` |
| `CustomTokenResponseClient(HttpServletRequest)` | `WebClientReactiveAuthorizationCodeTokenResponseClient` + parameters converter, wired via authentication managers |
| `CustomAuthRequestResolver` | `ServerOAuth2AuthorizationRequestResolver` delegating to `DefaultServerOAuth2AuthorizationRequestResolver` |
| `JwtDecoderFactory<ClientRegistration>` | `ReactiveJwtDecoderFactory<ClientRegistration>` set on the OIDC authentication manager |
| Manual `LogoutHandler` redirect with `id_token_hint` | `OidcClientInitiatedServerLogoutSuccessHandler` (does exactly this, correctly) |
| `SessionRegistry` + `maximumSessions(5)` | `ReactiveSessionRegistry` + `concurrentSessions(...)` (requires Spring Security ≥ 6.3, included in Boot 4.x) |
| `CookieSerializer` SameSite | `CookieWebSessionIdResolver.addCookieInitializer(...)` |
| `SessionCreationPolicy.ALWAYS`, `sessionFixation().newSession()`, `invalidSessionStrategy` | No direct equivalents. WebFlux creates the `WebSession` lazily, session IDs are rotated on authentication by default, and an invalid/expired session simply results in an unauthenticated exchange — which lands in your `ServerAuthenticationEntryPoint`, giving you the same forbidden-with-redirect behavior |
| `AuthenticationEntryPoint` / `AccessDeniedHandler` | `ServerAuthenticationEntryPoint` / `ServerAccessDeniedHandler` returning `Mono<Void>` |

Two caveats worth flagging: the `InMemoryReactiveSessionRegistry` and `InMemoryWebSessionStore` won't work across multiple gateway instances — for a clustered gateway, add Spring Session Redis and use `SpringSessionBackedReactiveSessionRegistry`. And since I can't see the bodies of your `Custom*` classes, I've left clearly marked `TODO` blocks where their specific logic (extra token parameters, JWT validation tweaks, etc.) needs to be ported — if you share those classes, I can convert them precisely too.
